                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "\"They want us to stay silent. But a camera can shout louder than a thousand voices.\"\n\nAs in current times as in times immemorial the Persian heartland, Iran, has always been a place of strife but also beauty and cultures.\n\nSo this little adventure game, or a interactive documentary I would rather say, named '1979 Revolution: Black Friday' by iNK Stories that came out in 2016 tells a personal and in-depth story how and why the 1978-79 revolution against the Shah and his regime occurred and leading up to faithful Friday. The game is full packed with information and history tidbits about the whole ordeal. \n\nThe music is made by the Iranian composer Nima Fakhrara and I believe this was his first video game soundtrack but not his last because he co-wrote the soundtrack to 'Warhammer 40,000: Space Marine 2'.\n\nThe voice actors in this game are all phenomenal. Not a weak performance anywhere. All of them are Iranian movie/tv actors that you have seen but never really remember if you know what I mean. Which is sad because they are all great as I said.\n\nGameplay wise it's fairly bareboned. It takes lessons from Telltale Games if you have ever played any of their games. Go there click there and sometimes quicktime events. Not overly engaging but the game is more about the vibes and the human tragedy that the Iranian people can never catch a break then it's about innovative gameplay. They had a story they wanted to tell. And considering most of the people who developed this game are of the Iranian diaspora it is an extremely personal game. One of the most personal games I've ever played honestly.  \n\nAnd it's a short little thing. You can be done with it in an afternoon and I would recommend it on sale. Gameplay it's very spartan and the visuals was not even good when the game came out but the game has an important culture importance in gaming at a whole. I think interactive documentaries should be more explored and would love to see more of them.",
